/target
# AND ANY NESTED ONE. `/target` is anchored at the root, so it does not match
# tests/support/target -- the support crate is its own package and cargo gives
# it its own build directory when it is built on its own (`cargo test -p
# fs-squashfs-test-support`). The sibling rust-fs-erofs committed 14 files and
# 2.2 MB that way; this repository escaped it only because the directory did
# not exist when the migration was committed.
target/
# Cargo.lock is intentionally tracked: this crate pins its dependencies and
# CI builds with `--locked`, so the resolved lockfile must be committed and
# kept in sync (the .githooks dependency gate enforces this).
*.img
*.sqfs
*audit*
.history

# Generated fixtures: oracle tests build SquashFS images into a tempdir,
# but guard against any stray image artifacts landing in the tree.
tests/fixtures/*.img
tests/fixtures/*.sqfs

# Committed offline fixtures: a tiny canonical gzip image (+ its meta) so
# `cargo test` can read a real SquashFS image even on a host without
# squashfs-tools installed. These are intentionally tracked; the blanket
# *.sqfs rule above would otherwise ignore them. The per-compressor +
# feature variants (squashfs-{xz,lz4,zstd,lzo,lzma,features}.sqfs) stay ignored
# — regenerate them with scripts/build-squashfs-feature-images.sh.
!test-disks/squashfs-basic.sqfs
!test-disks/*.meta.txt

# chore fingerprint cache (sources:/generates: checksums). Derived data.
.chore/

# Build output. `chore staticlib` writes the archive and headers here for a
# consumer to copy out of (`chore artifact` prints this directory).
/dist/
.DS_Store

# THE FUZZ CORPUS IS COMMITTED ON PURPOSE, and `*.img` above would
# otherwise swallow it silently. The images under fuzz/corpus/ are not
# build output or fixtures a script regenerates on demand -- they are
# the seeds the gate in tests/fuzz_decoders.rs replays and mutates, and
# without them that suite has nothing to run and says so only once CI
# has already gone red.
!fuzz/corpus/**

# THE SUITE'S SCRATCH DIRECTORY, AND IT IS INSIDE THE REPOSITORY ON
# PURPOSE. The oracle tools run in the fs-linux-test-harness VM, which sees
# this repository at the path the host knows it by and nothing else of the
# host -- so an image under /tmp is a path the tool asked to read it cannot
# open. scripts/test.sh and fs_squashfs_test_support::temp_dir both put
# scratch here, and the tier logs live in tmp/logs/.
/tmp/

# The harness's shared directory (fs-linux-test-harness.toml's [share]):
# what a run hands across to the guest and back.
/.vm-share/
